{"name":"Spider Cotton","hex":"#e2e8df","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spider","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various eight-legged, predatory arthropods, of the order Araneae, most of which spin webs to catch prey.","A program which follows links on the World Wide Web in order to gather information."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move like a spider.","To cover a surface like a cobweb."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spider"},{"term":"cotton","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fibrous substance:\n A soft, fibrous, usually white substance consisting of fine hairs, especially the substance around the seeds of a plant of genus Gossypium.\n Any similar soft, fibrous, white substance of fine hairs, of any origin.","A soft, fibrous, usually white substance consisting of fine hairs, especially the substance around the seeds of a plant of genus Gossypium."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of cotton."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cotton"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spider","blocks":[{"text":"Spiders are air-breathing arthropods that have eight limbs, chelicerae with fangs generally able to inject venom, and spinnerets that extrude silk. They are the largest order of arachnids and rank seventh in total species diversity among all orders of organisms. Spiders are found worldwide on every continent except Antarctica, and have become established in nearly every land habitat. As of January 2026, 53,680 spider species in 139 families have been recorded by taxonomists. However, there has been debate among scientists about how families should be classified, with over 20 different classifications proposed since 1900."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Araneus_diadematus_%28Clerck%2C_1757%29.JPG/330px-Araneus_diadematus_%28Clerck%2C_1757%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spider"},{"title":"Cotton","blocks":[{"text":"Cotton is a soft, fluffy staple fiber that grows in a boll, or protective case, around the seeds of the cotton plants of the genus Gossypium in the mallow family Malvaceae. The fiber is almost pure cellulose and may contain minor percentages of waxes, fats, pectins, and water. Under natural conditions, cotton bolls facilitate the dispersal of the seeds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/CottonPlant.JPG/330px-CottonPlant.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotton"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Palystes_castaneus_female_dorsum_numbered.jpg/500px-Palystes_castaneus_female_dorsum_numbered.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Palystes_castaneus_female_dorsum_numbered.jpg/1280px-Palystes_castaneus_female_dorsum_numbered.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Palystes_castaneus_female_dorsum_numbered.jpg/500px-Palystes_castaneus_female_dorsum_numbered.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APalystes_castaneus_female_dorsum_numbered.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Palystes_superciliosus_female_ventral_annotation_numbers.JPG/500px-Palystes_superciliosus_female_ventral_annotation_numbers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Palystes_superciliosus_female_ventral_annotation_numbers.JPG/1280px-Palystes_superciliosus_female_ventral_annotation_numbers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wi